<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Songtsen Gampo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 605–<a href="/years/649">649</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Songtsen Gampo was the emperor who built the Tibetan Empire in the early 7th century, ruling until 649 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> as his capital. He conquered Zhangzhung in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, married princesses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and, by tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, and is credited with introducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and a writing system based on Indian scripts. Tibetan tradition counts him as the first of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo.jpg" alt="Songtsen Gampo" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bhavna Sayana · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_King_Songtsen_Gampo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Songtsen Gampo is said to have been born at Gyama in Meldro, northeast of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, the son of the Yarlung king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namri-songtsen">Namri Songtsen</a> and a queen of the Tsépong clan. The dates of his birth and enthronement are uncertain. According to tradition he came to the throne about the age of thirteen, as the thirty-third king of the Yarlung dynasty, after his father was poisoned. The Old Book of Tang says he was still a minor when he succeeded. He had six consort queens, four Tibetan and two foreign, the Nepalese princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhrikuti">Bhrikuti</a> and the Chinese Princess Wencheng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Old Book of Tang records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>'s first embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 634, which brought an ultimatum demanding a marriage alliance. When it was refused, Tibet attacked Tang affiliates in 637 and 638 and threatened Songzhou with a large army, after which the Tang emperor granted the request. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-wencheng">Princess Wencheng</a>, a niece of Emperor Taizong, left China in 640 and arrived the next year, and peace between China and Tibet lasted for the rest of his reign. Chinese sources say he adopted Chinese dress, but the Tibetologist John Powers notes that Tibetan histories do not corroborate this.</p><p>Tibetan accounts tell that his sister Sadmarkar helped him defeat Lig-myi-rhya, king of Zhangzhung, and bring Zhangzhung into the empire in 645, giving him control of most or all of the plateau; the Old Book of Tang places its submission in 634, so the date is not settled. He moved the seat of his kingdom from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-valley">Yarlung Valley</a> to the Kyichu Valley, the site of future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang Temple</a> there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tradition credits his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thonmi-sambhota">Thonmi Sambhota</a> with creating the Tibetan script on a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, but the minister's existence is hard to verify and the script is now usually seen as developing later. Songtsen Gampo is traditionally the first of the three Dharma Kings, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralpachen">Ralpachen</a>, and is said to have built many temples, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a>, and the translation of Buddhist texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> into Tibetan began in his reign. Yet he showed little interest in spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> otherwise and was buried by pre-Buddhist rites. His tomb in the Chongyas Valley near Yalung is 13 metres high and 130 metres long.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Songtsen Gampo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The record begins with Buddhism: the Tibetan king <b>Songtsen Gampo</b> (reigned 627–649) extended the Tibetan Empire into Bhutan and ordered the temples of Jambay Lhakhang in Bumthang and Kyichu Lhakhang in the Paro Valley, and in 746 the faith was propagated in earnest under the exiled Indian king…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Namri Songtsen was poisoned, and his son <b>Songtsen Gampo</b>, who probably came to the throne as a youth around 618 or somewhat later, suppressed revolts by the conquered clans and made Lhasa his seat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Songtsen Gampo</b> allied with Zhangzhung by marrying his sister to its king, Lig Myirhya, then conquered it around 644, making it a province of the Tibetan Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhangzhung">Zhangzhung</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lhasa, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.14&amp;year=633" aria-label="Lhasa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6922 1081 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Songtsen Gampo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7372 1351h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7372 1351h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.14&amp;year=633">What was here in 633 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
